% A program may define a type whose NAME an exposed module also defines at a
% DIFFERENT ARITY.  NetMsg/1 here and NetMsg/0 in net.glp, which this self.glp
% exposes, are two type constructors, not two spellings of one.  The pairing is
% not contrived: programs/system/mad_predicates.glp carried NetMsg/0 into every
% program while the root self.glp exposed it, programs/social/spm/{cva,gsg,secure_gsg}
% define NetMsg/1, and that pairing stopped all three loading.  The linked
% program's type definitions used to be keyed by bare name, so whichever arity
% the directory walk reached second was dropped and every reference to it failed
% to resolve — here net.glp's NetStream would lose its element type.  GREEN, and
% it must stay green whichever order the walk reaches the two definitions in.
-expose(net).

NetMsg(C) ::= wrap(C).
NetMsgList(C) ::= [] ; [NetMsg(C) | NetMsgList(C)].
imported procedure code#go(NetMsgList(Constant)?).
exported procedure go(NetMsgList(Constant)?).
go(X) :- code # go(X?).
